                                    <h2><span class="shaft"><img id="1336-0" src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/icons/patterns/icon_sc.png" width=27 height=27 alt="Supercardioid" title="Supercardioid" ></span>Sennheiser Electronics Corporation MKE 400</h2>
                    
                    <h2 class="sub">                    Condenser Microphone</h2>
                    <p>The MKE 400 is a compact camera-mount shotgun microphone.</p><p>The mic mounts to DSLR or camcorder via hot or cold shoe mounts. A 200mm coiled cable with 3.5mm stereo plug connects the audio output to the camera&#8217;s audio input.</p><p>The mic is powered by 1 AAA battery; Sennheiser claims the batter has a 300 hour life. Switches on the mic body provide wind filter (low cut) and a high sensitivity mode.</p><p>The mic is extremely small (about 6 inches long) and lightweight (only 60gm).</p><blockquote><p><cite> Sam Malley 2011</cite><br />[compared to <a href="http://recordinghacks.com/microphones/Rode/VideoMic-Pro">Rode VideoMic Pro</a> outdoors]&#8230;neither microphone could handle wind. The outdoor shots that I included in the video were by far the best sounding ones I had. Most of the takes were a distorted mess&#8230; none of the softie windscreens or wind filter switches on the mics could cut it. The moral of the story? If you&#8217;re shooting in real wind, you&#8217;ll need a professional microphone with a full-blown zeppelin.</p></blockquote><p><p>The <a href="//recordinghacks.com/microphones/Sennheiser/MKE-400">Sennheiser Electronics Corporation MKE 400</a> is also known as: MKE400.</p>
